# How git should combine the two files every parallel branch touches. They
# collide for different reasons and the right answers are opposites.

# docs/decisions.md is append-only by construction: an entry is a new section
# at the end, so two branches that each add one are not in conflict, they are
# in sequence — and git only stops because both hunks end at the last line.
# `union` keeps both sides. What it gives up is the order the two entries land
# in, which is the one property an append-only log of dated decisions does not
# depend on; each entry carries its own date and issue number.
#
# This is the wrong driver for any file where two branches can edit the *same*
# line, because it would keep both versions of it and call that a merge. It is
# set here, on this file, for that reason and not as a general policy.
docs/decisions.md merge=union

# The render baselines are the opposite case. Each is a set of measured frame
# signatures; a textual merge of two of them yields valid JSON describing a
# render no machine ever produced, which then passes review precisely because
# it "merged cleanly". `-merge` makes git refuse: it leaves the current
# branch's version in place and marks the path conflicted, with no markers
# written into the JSON.
#
# Resolving one means re-measuring, never editing. Rebase, run the suite, and
# copy the run's own render-signatures-actual.json over the file — the command
# Tools/test.sh prints when the baseline moves. The unsuffixed file is the
# current runtime's; -ios<major> is that lane's. A visual change moves both,
# so approve both in the same commit.
RenderTests/Baselines/render-signatures.json -merge
RenderTests/Baselines/render-signatures-ios*.json -merge
